        "content": "The image contains a single problem (no diagram). Let me solve it.\n\n**Part (1):** In △ABC, AB = 3, BC = 2√3, cos B = √3/3.\n\nBy law of cosines: AC² = AB² + BC² − 2·AB·BC·cos B = 9 + 12 − 2·3·2√3·(√3/3) = 21 − 12 = 9, so AC = 3.\n\nThen cos A = (AB² + AC² − BC²)/(2·AB·AC) = (9 + 9 − 12)/(2·3·3) = 6/18 = 1/3.\n\n**Part (2):** D on ray BA beyond A, DE ∥ BC, AE ⊥ AC, DE = √6. Find CE.\n\nSince DE ∥ BC and D on line BA, triangles ADE and ABC are similar (A common angle, DE ∥ BC). So AD/AB = DE/BC = AE/AC.\n\nDE/BC = √6/(2√3) = √2/2. So AD = AB·(√2/2) = 3√2/2, AE = AC·(√2/2) = 3√2/2.\n\nNow AE ⊥ AC, so triangle ACE is right-angled at A. CE² = AE² + AC² = (3√2/2)² + 3² = 9/2 + 9 = 27/2. So CE = 3√6/2.\n\nLet me verify the similarity direction: D is on ray BA beyond A, so A lies between B and D. DE ∥ BC. In triangle ADE, angle DAE = angle BAC (same angle at A, since AD along AB). DE ∥ BC gives angle ADE = angle ABC. So △ADE ~ △ABC with ratio AD/AB = AE/AC = DE/BC. ✓",
